2026-11-27|Maximum Number of Items Within Budget


Question:

You are given a 2D integer array `items`, where `items[i] = [factor, price]`, and an integer `budget`.

There are unlimited copies of every item available for purchase.

For every item type purchased at least once, you receive one free copy of every other item whose factor is divisible by its factor.

Buying additional copies of the same item does not provide additional free copies. However, an item can be received for free from multiple different purchased item types.

Return the maximum total number of item copies that can be obtained while spending at most `budget`.

Approach:

We can divide this problem into two parts:

- First purchase of an item: Provides one purchased copy along with all eligible free copies.
- Additional purchases of an item: Provide only one purchased copy per purchase.

We will combine the 0/1 Knapsack algorithm with a greedy approach.

1. Calculate the number of free copies for every item.

   Create an array `freeCount` of size `n`, initially containing zeros.

   For every item `i`, check all other items `j`.

   If:

   `i != j && items[j][0] % items[i][0] == 0`

   Then purchasing item `i` provides one free copy of item `j`.

   Therefore:

   `freeCount[i]++`

   The total benefit of purchasing item `i` for the first time is:

   `benefit[i] = 1 + freeCount[i]`

   Here, 1 represents the purchased copy, while `freeCount[i]` represents the free copies.
2. Find the cheapest item.

   Once the first-purchase benefits have been considered, buying additional copies of any item provides exactly one purchased copy.

   Therefore, to maximize the number of additional copies, we should always purchase the cheapest available item.

   Let:

   `minPrice = minimum price among all items`

   With a remaining budget of `b`, we can purchase:

   `b / minPrice`

   additional copies.
3. Create a dynamic programming array.

   Create a DP array of size `budget + 1`.

   `dp[b]` represents the maximum number of copies obtainable within budget `b` using the cheapest-item purchases and the first-purchase benefits processed so far.
4. Initialize the DP array using the greedy approach.

   Before considering any first-purchase benefits, initialize:

   `dp[b] = b / minPrice`

   This represents the number of copies we can purchase by spending the budget on the cheapest item.

   Any free copies from these initial purchases are not yet counted.
5. Apply the 0/1 Knapsack algorithm.

   For each item, we have two choices:

   Choice 1: Do not include its first-purchase benefit.

   `dp[b]` remains unchanged.

   Choice 2: Purchase the item and include its first-purchase benefit.

   `dp[b - price] + 1 + freeCount[i]`

   We select whichever choice provides more copies.

   The DP transition becomes:

   `dp[b] = max(dp[b], dp[b - price] + 1 + freeCount[i])`
6. Traverse the budget backward.

   For every item, traverse the budget from `budget` down to its price.

   We traverse backward because the free-copy benefit of each item type can be obtained only once.

   Traversing forward could incorrectly allow the same first-purchase benefit to be counted multiple times.

   This is the main reason we use 0/1 Knapsack instead of ordinary Unbounded Knapsack for the first-purchase benefits.
7. Return the final answer.

   After processing every item, `dp[budget]` contains the maximum number of copies we can obtain.

Solution:

```
class Solution {
public:
    long long maxItems(vector<vector<int>>& items, int budget) {
        int n = items.size();

        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<int> freeCount(n, 0);
        int minPrice = INT_MAX;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            minPrice = min(minPrice, items[i][1]);

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (i != j &&
                    items[j][0] % items[i][0] == 0) {
                    freeCount[i]++;
                }
            }
        }

        vector<long long> dp(budget + 1, 0);

        for (int b = 0; b <= budget; b++) {
            dp[b] = b / minPrice;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int price = items[i][1];
            int benefit = 1 + freeCount[i];

            for (int b = budget; b >= price; b--) {
                dp[b] = max(dp[b],
                            dp[b - price] + benefit);
            }
        }

        return dp[budget];
    }
};
```

